Let {DG} be the annex to {AD}. Then {AG} and {GD|DG} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only, and the annex {DG} is commensurable with the rational straight line {AC} set out, while the square on the whole {AG} is greater than the square on the annex {GD|DG} by the square on a straight line commensurable in length with {AG}. [!just X.73; X.Def.III.2]

Since the square on {AG} is greater than the square on {GD|DG} by the square on a straight line commensurable with {AG}, therefore, if there is applied to {AG} a parallelogram equal to the fourth part of the square on {GD|DG} and deficient by a square figure, then it divides it into commensurable parts. [!just X.17]

Bisect, then, {DG} at {E}, apply to {AG} a parallelogram equal to the square on {EG} and deficient by a square figure, and let it be the rectangle {AF} by {FG}. Then {AF} is commensurable in length with {FG}.

Therefore {AG} is also commensurable in length with each of the straight lines {AF} and {FG}. [!just X.15]

But {AG} is rational and incommensurable in length with {AC}, therefore each of the straight lines {AF} and {FG} is also rational and incommensurable in length with {AC}. Therefore each of the rectangle {AI} by {FK} is medial. [!just X.13; X.21]

Again, since {DE} is commensurable with {EG}, therefore {DG} is also commensurable with each of the straight lines {DE} and {EG}. [!just X.15]

But {DG} is commensurable in length with {AC}.

Therefore each of the rectangles {DH} and {EK} is rational. [!just X.19]

Construct the square {LM} equal to {AI}, and subtract {NO}, equal to {FK}, about the same angle with {LM}, namely the angle {LPM|angP}. Then the squares {LM} and {NO} are about the same diameter. [!just VI.26]

Let {PR|RP} be their diameter, and draw the figure.

Since {AI} and {FK} are medial and equal the squares on {LP} and {PN}, the squares on {LP} and {PN} are also medial, therefore {LP} and {PN} are also medial straight lines commensurable in square only.

Since the rectangle {AF} by {FG} equals the square on {EG}, therefore {AF} is to {EG} as {EG} is to {FG}, while {AF} is to {EG} as {AI} is to {EK}, and {EG} is to {FG} as {EK} is to {FK}. Therefore {EK} is a mean proportional between {AI} and {FK}. [!just VI.17; VI.1; V.11]

But {MN} is also a mean proportional between the squares {LM} and {NO}, and {AI} equals {LM} while {FK} equals {NO}, therefore {MN} also equals {EK}.

But {DH} equals {EK}, and {LO} equals {MN}, therefore the whole {DK} equals the gnomon {UVW|UVWg} and {NO}.

Since, then, the whole {AK} equals {LM} and {NO}, and, in these, {DK} equals the gnomon {UVW|UVWg} and {NO}, therefore the remainder {AB} equals {TS}.

But {TS} is the square on {LN}, therefore the square on {LN} equals the area {AB}. Therefore {LN} is the side of the area {AB}.

I say that {LN} is a first apotome of a medial straight line.

Since {EK} is rational and equals {LO}, therefore {LO}, that is, the rectangle {LP} by {PN}, is rational.

But {NO} was proved medial, therefore {LO} is incommensurable with {NO}.

But {LO} is to {NO} as {LP} is to {PN}, therefore {LP} and {PN} are incommensurable in length. [!just VI.1, X.11]

Therefore {LP} and {PN} are medial straight lines commensurable in square only, which contain a rational rectangle. Therefore {LN} is a first apotome of a medial straight line. [!just X.74]

And it is the side of the area {AB}.

Therefore the side of the area {AB} is a first apotome of a medial straight line.

Therefore, *if an area is contained by a rational straight line and a second apotome, then the side of the area is a first apotome of a medial straight line.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
